{:db/id 17592186064168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186064039 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/id #uuid "818f4fbe-e251-57bd-90ae-b6517c6f2f89" :question/math-academy-id "e-367" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt x$." :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the square root as a rational power,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt x= x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\sqrt{x}dx &= \\intx^{1/2}dx \\\\\n&= (x^{1/2 + 1})/((\\frac{1}{2} + 1)) + C \\\\\n&= (x^{3/2})/((\\frac{3}{2})) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3} + C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2x^{3/2}}{3}) + \\frac{d}{dx}(C) &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3/2}) + 0 &=  \\\\\n\\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x^{3/2 - 1} &=  \\\\\n&= x^{1/2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186064557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e39638-07a3-50d9-bfe6-b9af29e98dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x^{4/3}}{3} + C" :db/id 17592186061538} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5c2097-f7f5-52e3-93b2-0e2d1df16d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x^{5/3}}{3} + C" :db/id 17592186061539} {:answer/id #uuid "267a13d7-f608-57fd-b791-75b0ac04fdbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{5/3}" :db/id 17592186061540} {:answer/id #uuid "74062014-ff92-54e2-84b6-a0ecdfafcc45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^{5/3}}{5} + C" :db/id 17592186061541} {:answer/id #uuid "7d652c0c-6ec0-53ed-9aeb-8cd0cb05022e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^{4/3}}{4} + C" :db/id 17592186061542}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4372205a-8138-530c-99c4-421ebef58c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186061537}] :question/id #uuid "cb61c7fc-a0d3-5742-be73-cb3d6f6a29fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-2023" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x^{2}}$."} {:db/id 17592186064713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a28cfe9c-9cdf-5268-9479-19d8fc77278a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x^{4/3}}{4} + C" :db/id 17592186062336} {:answer/id #uuid "e5ed8564-5cad-55ed-a076-d9dc26d59ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7x^{-7}}{6} + C" :db/id 17592186062337} {:answer/id #uuid "281cbfcf-2393-52a7-bfc4-1b6800c1f486" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{-7} + C" :db/id 17592186062338} {:answer/id #uuid "5d542b13-81e1-5fbb-a906-4a70b720de9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x^{4/3}}{3} + C" :db/id 17592186062339} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4520ae-938b-5eb0-b58c-c3d7e164cf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x^{6}}{7} + C" :db/id 17592186062340}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cb8ddff3-6382-5212-b6d5-a416c8499d35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186062335}] :question/id #uuid "176b300c-1630-58d7-aef2-06d5bc498dd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-29635" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $x^{1/3}$."} {:db/id 17592186233176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "353f4779-4251-5a93-b99f-1eda6757b24d" :question/math-academy-id "q-113674" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{1/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/4+1}}{(\\frac{1}{4}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5/4}}{(\\frac{5}{4})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4{x}^{5/4}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5/4})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}{x}^{5/4-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186233177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1eab1e18-d0df-58e6-917b-50a1d74ed3f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-113680" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}={x}^{3/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{3}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3/5+1}}{(\\frac{3}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{8/5}}{(\\frac{8}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{8/5}}{8})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{8/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{5}{x}^{8/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{3/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186233178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "63703f37-ab84-53aa-922b-b2e419a9f1a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-113695" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}={x}^{2/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{2}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{2/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2/5+1}}{(\\frac{2}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5}}{(\\frac{7}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{7/5}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{5}{x}^{7/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{2/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186233472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id 17592186232204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716b9760-7403-5664-a541-6f49fb28dac8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}" :db/id 17592186232204} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e017c6d-4e9c-5cb6-a364-f311476d6242" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186232203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a718be7-eb73-54f8-925e-728120665d52" :question/math-academy-id "q-143041" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[3]{x}$ equals{{field-1}} $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[3]{x}={x}^{1/3}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/3+1}}{(\\frac{1}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4/3}}{(\\frac{4}{3})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3{x}^{4/3}}{4})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4/3})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{4/3-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186234298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c9da781-1702-5ac7-9948-600ab4559823" :question/math-academy-id "q-71096" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{(\\frac{7}{2})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id 17592186235444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "85af4ca3-5e66-522a-9e5a-6efb1c7114ba" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id 17592186234897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id 17592186234897} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa3c58-8fd9-5d31-988a-048384e9e06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186235265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cee13c2e-6226-5f1c-9ea2-cd4611314d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-167564" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{(\\frac{6}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} & = \\\\ {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Radical Function"}
